  "reactionVersion": 2,
  "mechanismSummary": "Before removing the iodine, Corey acetylated the free OH (acetic anhydride, pyridine); a later version used a 4-phenylbenzoate instead. Tributyltin hydride and a little AIBN then remove the iodine by a radical chain. AIBN loses N₂ on heating, and its radical takes H from Bu₃SnH to give Bu₃Sn• (initiation, not drawn here). The tin radical abstracts the iodine atom from the lactone, a strong Sn–I bond for a weak C–I bond, and the carbon radical takes a hydrogen atom from Bu₃SnH, regenerating Bu₃Sn•. The iodine was only ever a handle for closing the lactone on the right face; it now leaves a CH₂. The other stereocentres are untouched (explained, not yet checked by the app). Removing the methyl ether (BBr₃) and oxidising the CH₂OH to an aldehyde (Collins reagent, CrO₃·pyridine) come next and are not drawn.",
